    "updated_at": "2026-08-21T11:30:41Z",
    "body": "You're right, it does. So the spec needs to define the coalescing/batching that browsers seem to do, replacing the current spec's single-character-at-a-time token-based approach.\n\n<details>\n<summary>ChatGPT 5.6 Sol Extra High's suggestion after analyzing browser code</summary>\n\nThe complete browser-matching fix is to specify a pending parser-text buffer and flush it using DOM operations.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\npending parser text = { insertion location, data }\n```\n\nWhen “insert a character” runs:\n\n1. If its insertion location differs from the pending one, flush the pending text.\n1. Append the character to the pending data instead of immediately touching the DOM.\n\nFlush pending text:\n\n1. If a `Text` node immediately precedes the stored insertion location, [[replace data](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-cd-replace)](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-cd-replace) once, appending the entire buffered string.\n1. Otherwise, create one `Text` node containing the entire string and insert it.\n1. Clear the buffer.\n\nA flush must happen:\n\n- Before processing any non-character token, even an ignored token.\n- When the adjusted insertion location changes.\n- Before executing script or otherwise allowing parser reentrancy.\n- At EOF.\n- Before the parser pauses or yields such that DOM changes must become observable.\n\nThis explains the tested Chromium behavior:\n\n| Input | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `a b c d` | One run creates a text node: zero `characterData` records |\n| `a&amp;b` | Character reference remains in the run: zero records |\n| `a</bogus>b` | Ignored end tag splits the runs: one record |\n| `a<script>…remove()…</script>b` | Script splits the runs: one record |\n\nThe flush’s use of replace data supplies exactly one mutation record, children-changed steps, and range maintenance when appending to an existing node.\n\nThis does not require rewriting every tokenizer state: the buffering can sit between “insert a character” and the DOM. But it does require new parser state and carefully defined flush points. Blink and Gecko already have analogous pending-text buffers in their implementations: [Blink’s `pending_text_`](https://github.com/chromium/chromium/blob/main/third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/parser/html_construction_site.cc) and [Gecko’s `charBuffer`](https://github.com/mozilla/gecko-dev/blob/master/parser/html/nsHtml5TreeBuilder.cpp).\n\nSo yes: to match browsers entirely, the spec change is substantially more than replacing one sentence with “replace data.” It needs to standardize parser text batching as well.\n</details>",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
